# HANBI — "Things Left Unsaid"
# Route theme: being understood without speaking is beautiful, but sometimes feelings still need to be spoken.
# Ladder: low — water before you know you're thirsty | mid — a doodle instead of an answer | high — says it out loud.
# Seed: var.color (blue / yellow / green / red) — set in hanbi_intro, paid off in the room, the margins, the portrait.
# Visual: the Memory Room is line art (mem_lineart); colour arrives only when something is said aloud (mem_color).

=== hanbi_intro
@show hanbi neutral at=c
> Hanbi has taken the end of the sagging sofa furthest from the Archive Wall, as if he wanted to be able to see all of it at once.
> A sketchbook rests on his knee. The pencil from behind his ear is in his hand now, moving in short, unhurried strokes.
> On the armrest beside him, water bottles stand in a neat row, like he's expecting company.
> He glances up. Then, without a word, he holds one out to you.
> You didn't know you were thirsty until your hand closes around it.
you: …Thanks. Did you buy one for everyone?
hanbi: There were eight left in the machine.
> Seven on the armrest. One in your hand. Nine people in the lobby, counting you.
> He goes back to his drawing before you can do the arithmetic out loud.
* [sincere] "This one was yours, wasn't it."
    @rel hanbi trust+1
    hanbi: …I'm not thirsty.
    > He says it the way people say *it's nothing* — like a door being closed very gently, so it won't wake anyone.
* [playful] Crack it open, take a sip, and hold it out to him.
    @rel hanbi closeness+1
    > He looks at the bottle. Then at you. Then he takes it, drinks exactly one polite mouthful, and hands it back.
    hanbi[shy]: …Okay. Half.
* [quiet] Sit on the other end of the sofa. Don't make it a thing.
    @rel hanbi trust+1
    > You sit. You drink. He seems to like that better than a thank-you.
> His page is the lobby. The front desk, the sofas, the Archive Wall — every glass box drawn in, hundreds of tiny squares.
> All pencil. Beside him on the cushion, a small tin lies open: four coloured pencils, each worn down to a different length.
hanbi: …I only let myself use one colour. On a drawing like this. I can't decide.
> He tips the tin towards you, a very small amount. It's not quite asking. It's leaving room for you to answer.
* [quiet] Blue. For the glow on the wall.
    @var color = "blue"
    > He shades it in box by box, so lightly the paper barely notices.
    hanbi: …Everyone looks at that first anyway. It should be right.
* [playful] Yellow. For the laundromat light through the window.
    @var color = "yellow"
    hanbi[smile]: …The laundromat.
    hanbi: It's always on. I like that it's always on.
* [curious] Green. For the exit sign.
    @var color = "green"
    hanbi[surprised]: …The exit sign?
    you: It's the only thing in here that's been doing its job all night.
    hanbi[laugh]: …Okay. Yes.
* [sincere] Red. For the button on the vending machine.
    @var color = "red"
    > He looks out through the glass door at the machine, then back at his page, and adds one dot of red no bigger than a grain of rice.
    hanbi: …Small. But it's the first thing you see.
@show charlie smile at=l
charlie: Hyung. Hyung. Does anybody have a—
> Hanbi is already holding up a phone charger. He doesn't look away from the page.
charlie[surprised]: …How did you know?
hanbi: You've been at four percent since the alley.
@show songha laugh at=r
songha: He's the only one who ever brings one. He brought one to the beach once, {{playerName}}. The *beach*.
hanbi: …There was a café.
songha: Three kilometres away.
@hide charlie songha
you: Did you send something? To the archive?
hanbi[thinking]: …A drawing. I think.
hanbi: It wasn't finished.
you: Then why send it?
hanbi: …I thought future me would know how to finish it.
hanbi[smile]: …He doesn't. Yet.
> He goes back to the page. It's the kind of quiet that isn't asking you to leave.
@hub act1

=== hanbi_intro_repeat
@show hanbi smile at=c
> Hanbi's still drawing. The colour you picked is on the page now, laid down so lightly it looks like breath on glass.
> Without looking up, he moves the water bottles over so there's room for you on the armrest.
@hub act1

=== hanbi_door
@bg studio_hall t=fade
@show hanbi neutral at=c
> RECORD 01. The first door in the hall, and the plainest. Hanbi is already standing in front of it, hands in his coat pockets.
> He isn't looking at the door. He's looking at the floor just in front of it, like a line he's thinking about crossing.
@textbox echo
echo: RECORD 01 — OWNER: HANBI. STATUS: SEALED. AWAITING CONSENT.
@textbox normal
hanbi: …It's the drawing. I told you.
hanbi: It's just— it isn't very good. I was sixteen. And it's not…
> He takes the pencil from behind his ear, turns it over once, and puts it back.
you: Not what?
hanbi[thinking]: …Done.
* [sincere] "You don't have to show me."
    @rel hanbi trust+1
    hanbi: I know.
    hanbi[smile]: That's why it's okay.
* [playful] "I've seen your lobby drawing. I'm braced for greatness."
    @rel hanbi closeness+1
    hanbi[shy]: …Now I'm nervous.
* [quiet] Wait with him.
    @rel hanbi trust+1
    > Neither of you says anything. After a while, his shoulders come down a little.
> He takes one hand out of his pocket and rests it flat against the door. Not pushing. Just resting there, the way you'd rest a hand on a sleeping animal.
hanbi: …ECHO. It's okay.
hanbi: Yes.
@textbox echo
echo: Consent received. The door will open for whoever the owner is standing beside.
@textbox normal
> He's standing beside you. He doesn't point this out. He just doesn't move.
* "Open it."
    @goto hanbi_room
* "Not yet."
    @rel hanbi trust+1
    hanbi[smile]: …Okay.
    > He sits down on the floor with his back against the door, the way people sit outside a room where someone is sleeping.
    hanbi: I'm good at waiting. It's kept for six years.
    hanbi: …It'll keep a little longer.
    @hub act2

=== hanbi_room
@bg mem_lineart t=memory
@music hanbi
@fx sketch n=1
@amb rain
@textbox echo
echo: RECORD 01 — OWNER: HANBI. TYPE: DRAWING, PENCIL ON PAPER. DATE: FEBRUARY, SIX YEARS AGO. 15:40. UNRESOLVED SUBJECTS: 1.
echo: Note: the record contains no colour data. Reconstruction is limited to line.
@textbox normal
@cam reveal
> The door opens onto paper.
> It's the lounge. Studio 25's lounge, at the back of the ground floor — but drawn. Every edge is a pencil line. Every shadow is hatching.
> The sofas are outlines. The rain on the window is a hundred short, slanted strokes.
> Even the air has a grain to it, like the tooth of good paper.
> Somewhere, very faintly, you can hear rain. Drawn rain shouldn't make a sound. This does.
@show hanbi surprised at=c
hanbi: …Oh.
hanbi: This is— this is how I saw it. Then.
> He walks forward slowly. Where his shoes touch the floor, the floor grows a little more certain: a line darkens, a board gains a knot.
* [playful] "You drew the rain one drop at a time?"
    @rel hanbi closeness+1
    hanbi[shy]: …It was a long afternoon.
    hanbi: There was a lot of rain.
* [sincere] "Hanbi. It's beautiful."
    @rel hanbi trust+1
    > He doesn't answer. The tips of his ears go faintly pink — the only colour anywhere in the room.
* [quiet] Say nothing. Walk the room with him.
    @rel hanbi trust+1
    > You fall into step beside him. He notices, and slows down so you don't have to.
> By the window stands a desk. You know it before your brain catches up: FUTURE LETTER, hand-lettered in capitals, taped to the front.
> Someone is sitting at it. A figure made of light instead of line, soft at the edges, bent over a sheet of paper. Writing.
> Where the face should be, there's nothing. Not shadow. Not a blur. Just paper nobody drew on.
@if rooms() == 0
    hanbi[thinking]: …ECHO. Who is that?
    @textbox echo
    echo: Unresolved subject. The owner did not complete them.
    @textbox normal
    hanbi: …That's fair.
    hanbi: I never knew their name. They were just… there. At the desk. All afternoon.
    > The figure writes slowly. Crosses something out. Writes it again.
    > Your chest goes tight, and you don't know why. It's a drawing of a stranger.
@elif rooms() >= 4
    hanbi[smile]: …Is that you again?
    you: Why does everyone assume it's me?
    hanbi: Because it's been you. Every time I've heard about.
    hanbi: You were a very… busy kid.
    > It's the closest thing to a joke he's made all night. He looks quietly pleased with it.
@else
    hanbi[thinking]: …Is it you?
    hanbi: The others said— in their rooms, there was someone. Made of light.
    you: I don't know yet.
    hanbi: …Okay. We'll look. Slowly.
@hide all
@textbox memory
> The rain gets louder. The lines of the room thicken, settle — and the room is full.
> A man asleep over a guitar case. A woman whispering into her phone by the door. Two kids sharing a cup of noodles with one fork.
> And in the armchair in the corner, knees pulled up, a sketchbook balanced on them: a boy whose hair hasn't turned the colour of the sea yet.
> Sixteen. All elbows and quiet. He draws the man with the guitar. He draws the woman with the phone.
> He draws them fast and a little shyly, as if apologising to each of them for looking.
> Then he looks at the desk.
> The figure at the Future Letter desk writes, stops, crosses out. It jams a blunt pencil into the sharpener clamped to the corner and turns.
> The sharpener does nothing. It never did.
you: …Does anyone have a sharpener?
> The boy doesn't say anything. He gets up, crosses the room, and holds out his own pencil. Already sharp.
you: Oh. Thanks. I'll give it back.
hanbi: …Keep it.
> The figure glances at the sketchbook in his other hand.
you: Are you drawing everyone?
hanbi: …Just people who are waiting.
you: I'm not waiting. I'm writing.
hanbi: You look like you're waiting.
you: For what?
hanbi: …I don't know. That's why I haven't done your face yet.
> The figure laughs — short, surprised — and the boy looks at the floor like he's said far too much.
hanbi: What are you writing?
you: A letter. To me. For later.
hanbi: What does it say?
you: I haven't decided.
> He nods, as if that's a perfectly reasonable answer. He goes back to his armchair.
> He draws the desk. The paper. The hand holding his pencil. He saves the face for last. He always saves the face for last.
@sfx phone
> A phone buzzes on the desk. The figure reads it, and all the stillness goes out of their shoulders at once.
you: Sorry— I have to go. I have to go.
> They fold the letter and push it through the slot of the Future Letter box, jiggling it on the left side where it sticks.
> They're gone before the door finishes swinging. The pencil goes with them.
> In the corner, the boy looks down at a portrait with no face. The rain keeps going. He doesn't turn the page.
@textbox normal
@show hanbi thinking at=c
> The room goes quiet, except for the rain.
> You're staring at the letter box. At the slot that sticks on the left, which you know sticks on the left because you jiggled it every morning for a winter.
you: …That sharpener never worked.
hanbi: …What?
you: The one on the desk. I complained about it the whole winter. Nobody ever fixed it.
> Hanbi looks at you. Then at the figure of light, which has gone back to the beginning, writing its first line again.
hanbi: …You said you worked the desk.
you: One winter. I was sixteen.
you: I wrote my letter at that desk. With—
> You stop. You remember it all at once and much too clearly: the weight of a borrowed pencil. How sharp it was. How you meant to give it back.
you: …With somebody else's pencil.
hanbi[surprised]: …
hanbi: It's you.
@cam closeup hanbi
* [sincere] "It's me. I'm sorry I left before you finished."
    @rel hanbi trust+2
    hanbi: …You had to go. I saw.
    hanbi[sad]: I wasn't angry. I was just— left with a face I didn't have.
* [playful] "I think I lost your pencil. In a move. Several moves."
    @rel hanbi closeness+2
    hanbi[laugh]: …It was a bad pencil.
    hanbi: It smudged.
    you: I wrote my whole future with that pencil.
    hanbi[smile]: …Then it did okay.
* [quiet] Look at the figure at the desk for a long time.
    @rel hanbi trust+1 closeness+1
    > The figure crosses out a line and writes it again. You remember that line. You remember not being sure you were allowed to hope for it.
    > Hanbi doesn't interrupt. He just moves a little closer, so your sleeves almost touch.
@textbox echo
echo: Record updated. Unresolved subject: identified. Recipient {{playerName}}.
echo: Portrait status: incomplete. Colour data: none. The owner may supply colour from memory, if he wishes.
@textbox normal
hanbi[thinking]: …From memory.
> He looks at the sofa. Sits on the arm of it, carefully, as if it might still be only a drawing.
hanbi: It was orange. This sofa. A really— bad orange.
hanbi: Like a traffic cone that gave up.
@sfx pencil
> Colour seeps into the lines beneath him, slow as tea into a sugar cube: a tired, dusty, unmistakable orange.
you: Oh, I remember that sofa. Everyone hated that sofa.
hanbi[smile]: …I liked it.
hanbi: Nobody sat on it. So there was always somewhere to sit.
> Around the room, nothing else changes. The rain stays pencil. The desk stays white. The room seems to be waiting to be told.
hanbi: …I'm not good at this part.
you: What part?
hanbi: Saying the colours out loud. I used to just— put them in. Afterwards. When nobody was looking.
> He takes his sketchbook out of his coat — tonight's — and a coloured-pencil stub, and holds them both out to you.
hanbi: …Do you want to? You don't have to be good.
* [playful] "I'm going to be so bad at this."
    @rel hanbi closeness+1
    hanbi[laugh]: …Good.
* [sincere] "Only if you tell me what colours things were."
    @rel hanbi trust+1
    hanbi: …I'll try. Out loud.
* [romantic] Take the pencil, and sit on the orange sofa beside him. {if hanbi.closeness >= 2 or hanbi.trust >= 3}
    @rel hanbi closeness+1 romance+1
    > He shifts over without being asked. The sofa creaks exactly the way it used to. Some things ECHO gets perfectly right.
    hanbi[shy]: …Nobody used to sit here.
> When he opens the sketchbook, the page isn't blank. ECHO has put the room there, small: the window, the desk, the sofa, the rain, in fine grey line.
hanbi: The window was grey. Not— rain-grey. Blue-grey. The desk was the colour of old rice paper.
hanbi: The rest… I don't remember. Do what you want.
@minigame sketch
@if var.minigame_score >= 70
    > When you finish, the little room is careful and bright. You've stayed inside almost every line.
    hanbi[surprised]: …You're neat.
    you: Is that a compliment?
    hanbi[smile]: From me? …It's the best one.
@elif var.minigame_score >= 35
    > When you finish, the little room is bright in patches: the sofa very orange, the window very blue, the desk a colour you'd defend in court.
    hanbi[smile]: …You went outside the lines. Here. And here.
    hanbi: I like those parts best. That's where it looks like someone was actually there.
@else
    > When you finish, the little room is one enthusiastic storm of colour. You have, among other things, coloured the rain.
    hanbi[laugh]: …You coloured the rain.
    you: Was I not supposed to?
    hanbi: Nobody colours the rain. …I never thought of it.
    hanbi[smile]: I'm keeping this.
> Around you, the real room stirs. A curtain goes faintly blue. The noodle cup on the table turns red.
> Small things, tried on and taken off again, as if the room is still deciding.
> The figure at the desk stays light. The face stays paper.
you: Hanbi. Why did you send it? If it wasn't finished.
hanbi[thinking]: …
> He opens his mouth. Closes it. Picks up the pencil, like he's going to answer by drawing — the way he answers most things.
> Then, carefully, like setting down a cup filled to the brim, he puts the pencil down.
hanbi: Because I didn't want to guess.
hanbi: I could have made up a face. I do that, sometimes. With people who leave before I'm done.
hanbi: But then it's a face I decided you had. Not yours.
hanbi: So I left it. In case—
> The sentence stops. You wait. It doesn't start again.
* [sincere] "In case what?"
    @rel hanbi trust+1
    hanbi: …In case I saw you again.
    hanbi: And could just look.
* [quiet] Don't make him finish it.
    @rel hanbi trust+1
    > You let the sentence stay where it is. He glances at you, surprised, and something in his face eases.
    hanbi: …Thank you. Usually I have to say I'm fine before people stop asking.
* [curious] "Do you do that a lot? Leave things unfinished?"
    @rel hanbi closeness+1
    hanbi[smile]: …Sentences, mostly.
    hanbi: The others finish them for me. Songha's very good at it. He's wrong about half the time.
hanbi: …I always thought— if you really know someone, you don't need to say things. You just bring the water. They know.
hanbi: When I hand Charlie a charger, he knows what I mean.
you: What do you mean?
hanbi[shy]: …"You're going to lose your phone at two a.m. and panic. I'm here."
you: That's a lot to fit into a charger.
hanbi[laugh]: …It's a big charger.
> Then the laugh fades, and he looks at the faceless figure at the desk.
hanbi[sad]: But you left. And I never said anything. Not even my name.
hanbi: So for six years, you were the person I didn't say anything to.
> The drawn rain goes on falling past the window, one careful stroke at a time.
* [sincere] "You could say something now."
    @rel hanbi trust+1
    hanbi: …I know. I'm looking for the right one.
* [playful] Hold out your hand, formally. "Hi. I'm {{playerName}}. You lent me a pencil."
    @rel hanbi closeness+1
    > He looks at your hand. Then, very solemnly, he shakes it. His fingers are grey at the edges with graphite.
    hanbi[smile]: …Hanbi. You still owe me a pencil.
* [romantic] Sit beside him. Rest your hand on the cushion between you, palm up. Let him decide. {if hanbi.closeness >= 2 or hanbi.trust >= 3}
    @rel hanbi closeness+1 romance+1
    > You don't reach for him. You just sit down on the orange sofa and leave your hand there, open, on the cushion.
    > He looks at it for a long moment. Then he lays his hand in yours — lightly, the way you'd set down a pencil you meant to pick up again.
* [quiet] Wait. He'll find it.
    @rel hanbi trust+1
    > You wait. Somewhere under the rain, you can hear him deciding.
hanbi: …{{playerName}}.
> It's the first time he's said your name. He says it slowly, like he's checking the spelling.
hanbi: I'm glad it was you.
hanbi: At the desk. I'm really glad it was you.
@bg mem_color t=slow d=3
@sfx chime
@fx sketch n=0.5
> And the room fills.
> Not all at once. It runs, the way watercolour finds the wet parts of a page. The window goes blue-grey with real rain. The floorboards warm to honey.
> The noodle cup is red. The guitar case is green. The sofa is still, stubbornly, the worst orange in Seoul.
@if var.color == "yellow"
    > And the pencil in the hand of the figure at the desk — his pencil — turns laundromat yellow. The colour you picked in the lobby.
@elif var.color == "green"
    > And the pencil in the hand of the figure at the desk — his pencil — turns exit-sign green. The colour you picked in the lobby.
@elif var.color == "red"
    > And the pencil in the hand of the figure at the desk — his pencil — turns vending-machine red. The colour you picked in the lobby.
@else
    > And the pencil in the hand of the figure at the desk — his pencil — turns the blue of the Archive Wall. The colour you picked in the lobby.
@show hanbi surprised at=c
hanbi: …I don't remember what colour it was. The pencil.
hanbi[smile]: So I used yours.
> He looks around like someone who's walked into a room he painted and doesn't remember painting.
hanbi: …I said one thing.
you: You said it very well.
hanbi[shy]: It was five words.
you: Six, the second time.
hanbi[laugh]: …You counted.
> The figure at the desk still has no face. But it's sitting in a room full of colour now, writing with a borrowed pencil, and it looks less alone.
> Hanbi picks up the sketchbook from the armchair — the old one, the sixteen-year-old's — and opens it to the portrait.
> He holds up his pencil. Looks at you. Looks at the blank oval.
hanbi: …Not now. Not tonight, probably. I'm not— ready to decide.
hanbi: But can I look? So I remember. For later.
* [sincere] "Look as long as you want."
    @rel hanbi trust+1
    > He looks. Not the quick, apologetic glance he gives strangers. Long, and steady, and entirely unhidden.
* [playful] "Do I have to make a serious face?"
    @rel hanbi closeness+1
    hanbi[laugh]: …Make whatever face. That's the point.
* [romantic] Turn towards him, and hold still. {if hanbi.trust >= 2}
    @rel hanbi romance+1
    > You sit the way people sit for portraits: chin up a little, hands in your lap, trying not to blink.
    > He doesn't draw. He only looks. It's much harder to hold still for than a pencil.
    hanbi[shy]: …You're allowed to breathe.
@textbox echo
echo: Record 01 updated. Colour data: supplied by the owner. Portrait status: incomplete.
echo: Owner, shall I mark the record as unresolved?
@textbox normal
hanbi: …No.
hanbi[smile]: Mark it as— in progress.
@textbox echo
echo: …Noted. That is a new status. I will allow it.
@textbox normal
> At the door, he stops and looks back: orange sofa, blue-grey rain, the figure of light still writing at the desk with a borrowed pencil.
hanbi: …Thank you. For coming in.
* [sincere] "Thank you for letting me."
    @rel hanbi trust+1
    hanbi[smile]: …You were already in it. I just opened the door.
* [playful] "Thanks for the pencil. Six years late."
    @rel hanbi closeness+1
    hanbi[smile]: …I'll send you an invoice.
* [romantic] Stay by the door a moment longer, beside him. {if hanbi.romance >= 1}
    @rel hanbi romance+1
    > Neither of you reaches for the handle. For a few seconds you both just stand there, looking at a room you've been in twice, six years apart.
    hanbi: …Okay.
    hanbi[shy]: Now.
